# General Debate on the 1968–69 Budget Statement
Tamil title: 1968–69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான நிதிநிலை அறிக்கைமீது பொது விவாதம்

Speech 35, 6 March 1968

**Honourable C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Speaker, I sincerely regret that for several days I could not participate in the proceedings of the House from which I have drawn great inspiration and delight. Whenever I falter, the views offered by members of different parties in this House and the other House give me strength. Whenever I have doubts about an issue, their arguments help me resolve them. I therefore deeply regret my temporary inability to attend these sittings. I learned from the newspapers that members were concerned, and I thank them.

In particular, our friend the Leader of the Opposition congratulated this Ministry on completing one year and wished it another four years. I have no doubt that, as my friend, he would feel that way. But I owe him special thanks for wishing it as a Congressman.

As I said at the outset, we did not assume office with an itch to remain for five or ten years. Nevertheless, his wish that we continue encourages us to discharge our responsibilities more effectively, and I thank him for it.

Outside this House, the respected Kamaraj, formerly President of the All India Congress, said that if the Union Government caused any danger to this Government he would personally prevent it. This was discussed here. When Mr Karuthiruman said that our respected friend M. P. Sivagnanam had asked, “Who is he to say that?”, I was startled. Knowing M. P. Sivagnanam's nature, I worried whether he could have said such a thing. But when his actual speech was read, it became clear that he was distinguishing democratic principle from efforts by an individual to safeguard democracy, not speaking in the sense alleged.

The Leader of the Opposition then spoke at length of what a great leader Kamaraj is. Perhaps he thought people would understand Kamaraj correctly only if he spoke of him. His affection is natural and just; I am happy to tell the House that those of us here share it. Kamaraj has earned the affection of poor working people in every party. But praising him excessively during a debate may tempt someone on the other side to say four contrary words about him. That is why I think the matter need not have been carried so far.

The Budget Statement has fifty-eight pages. Nearly four explain relations between the Union and the States. I first worried that the House might overlook those four pages, but every speaker, including the Leader of the Opposition, has shown that they performed their task very well.

I wish to give one clarification. When I discuss Union-State relations here or outside, I have never said the problem exists because Congress governs at the Centre while non-Congress parties govern some States. I have discussed general constitutional principles beyond party. Union-State relations are becoming a major political question, and I want every party to support its examination without partisanship.

The Leader of the Opposition said, “If the Congress Centre discriminates against non-Congress States, say so and we shall fight it.” I did not say yesterday, today, or ever that discrimination had occurred. On the contrary, the Union Government has treated me and our Government with considerable goodwill.

For example, some political leaders in this State alleged that the DMK instigated, supported, or participated in the national-flag-burning agitation. Yet Union Minister Shukla stated that the agitation had no connection with the DMK, was not organised by it, and was not instigated by it. I cite this not to explain the agitation but to demonstrate that those at the Centre did not act with prejudice toward us.

If you turn this into “Congress rules there and a non-Congress party here; therefore they clash,” you will miss the constitutional principle. India has a federal Constitution. In any federation, adjustment between the two levels is unavoidable: the question must be resolved repeatedly as circumstances change and powers are redistributed. I raised it so every member would accept that general principle, not as a Congress versus non-Congress quarrel.

In the opening pages I described the circumstances in which we took office. I said the Third Five-Year Plan had ended without reaching its targets and that we assumed responsibility at that difficult moment. Congress members became angry, interpreting criticism of the Plan as criticism of Congress rule. Their response now obliges me to describe the Plan's failures more fully.

My statement said only that more than the allotted money had been spent without reaching the targets, and that in this sense the Plan failed. I did not say there had been no development. The Leader of the Opposition said Congress had raised State revenue from Rs 80 crore to Rs 200 crore before handing it to us, and spoke of the taxes it had imposed. Continue that line and we must thank Rajaji for introducing sales tax; continue further and we must bow to the British for teaching governments to tax without fear. Since no one knows where that chain will end, I stop at a reasonable point.

The Third Plan proposed expenditure of roughly Rs 8,000 crore to achieve stated results. Did it? The OCR corrupts a series of English planning categories, but the numerical comparisons that follow are readable. The foodgrain target was 100 million tons; achievement was 72 million. The target for bringing new land under irrigation was 29 million acres; achievement was 18.6 million. Steel-ingot production was planned at 9.2 million tons and reached 6.2 million. Machine tools worth Rs 30 crore were planned; output was about Rs 23 crore. Nitrogenous fertiliser was targeted at 800,000 tons and reached 253,000. Coal was targeted at 97 million tons and reached 68 million. Mill cloth was targeted at 5,800 million yards and reached 4,400 million; handloom cloth was targeted at 3,500 million yards and reached 3,150 million. Tamil Nadu's large handloom contribution helped keep that shortfall relatively small.

Mr Karuthiruman may ask what barren statistics these are, so I stop there. But they plainly show that the Third Plan did not succeed.

He asks, “A year has passed; what employment have you created? What new industries have you begun?” I take this as concern for the people, not anger toward us. But he should consider whether it is a matter of pride that his party announced the Fourth Five-Year Plan to the world and still cannot implement it.

Congress once took pride in implementing successive plans whatever happened. Even when Rajaji warned that the country could not bear the Plan and should be given a holiday, Congress declared planning a serial story that could never stop. A draft Fourth Plan was circulated, yet it has not come into force.

There is a humorous story about a neglected wife who padded her stomach with folded gunny cloth to convince her husband she was pregnant. Month by month she added padding; in the eighth month she went to her mother's house and returned saying the child had been born and died. Students nicknamed the husband the “gunny-cloth teacher.” Likewise, the Fourth Plan was padded and displayed for so long that when its time came it collapsed of itself. Now we are told even the prenatal ceremony lies ahead. If Mr Karuthiruman considers that a distinction for his party or government, he may enjoy it.

I did not blame them because the failure was not theirs alone. Kamaraj himself, presiding over the Jaipur Congress, offered a practical view: a sky-measuring plan of Rs 22,000 or 25,000 crore would not work; the Fourth Plan should be about Rs 15,000 crore. Had Congress accepted that advice, the country would not have passed two years without a Plan. Industries providing jobs and expanded medical services might then have emerged.

Having stopped the Plan, they put the blame on me and ask what I achieved in one year. That recalls the man who murdered his parents and, when sentenced, pleaded with the judge, “Sir, I am an orphan; I have neither mother nor father, so do not kill me.” It is not right to disable the Plan and then plead helplessness when its failure is pointed out.

This is not political fever. I wished to quote economists outside politics who described the Third Plan's failure. Unfortunately, the long English quotations attributed in the scan to economist and Planning Commission Deputy Chairman D. R. Gadgil, former office-holder H. M. Patel, former Finance Minister T. T. Krishnamachari, and Dr Lokanathan are destroyed by Tamil-only OCR and cannot responsibly be reconstructed here. Their surrounding Tamil says that the Plan missed its targets and that the Government had lost effective command of the economic machinery.

Congress wants government to retain a grip on the economic mechanism; that is its fundamental difference from the Swatantra Party, which opposes such pressure. But control requires a skilled hand. A fencing teacher says that a sword must be held firmly yet with enough suppleness to bend and move. Too tight a grip prevents movement; too loose a grip sends the stroke astray. Skill of hand is decisive.

I referred to the Third Plan only to show that we took office amid the resulting economic recession. A man may marry a beautiful woman; if smallpox marks her face a year or two later, few husbands forget her former beauty. The Leader of the Opposition remembers the unmarked beauty; I confront the pockmarked face. The economy bears such marks.

A State Government's schemes can yield their full benefit only in relation to Union policy. Does that mean we will do nothing? Certainly not. The Budget Statement shows what we are trying to accomplish in this difficult interval. The respective ministers will answer detailed departmental points.

We hope larger employment opportunities can be created next year and after the Fourth Plan takes shape. If asked why we do not create them now, name the major industries Congress established solely from State finances. State resources cannot finance a heavy-boiler plant, Neyveli, or the Avadi tank factory. The large industries established here in fifteen years were begun by the Union Government or private capital. A State can establish smaller factories for bicycle assembly, pottery, mats, matches, or footwear, and we have done what our finances allow.

It is easy to announce an industry and hard to complete it. The previous Government's proposed Arakkonam factory remained on paper without even a signed agreement; we have now reached the point of concluding an agreement with the Soviet Government. The Rajapalayam cement factory is advancing rapidly. Seven cooperative tea factories have begun in the lower and upper Kotagiri areas, two more are being expanded, and private factories are also expanding.

Members cite the closure of Wimco and Metal Box. They should honestly recall how many mills in Coimbatore and Pudukkottai stood closed when they governed and what they did to reopen them. Seeing factories shut at 1:30 in the afternoon does not prove permanent closure; it is true only until they reopen. Conditions are developing for Wimco and the others to reopen.

If any State government is to establish major industries from its own finances, it must receive a fair share of revenue sources. When I said so, members replied that the States already receive seventy-five per cent of income tax. I did not mean merely a share of collected taxes. Successive Finance Commissions have increased that share, and I do not deny it. But the Centre created company tax and retained it entirely, though company income had once contributed to divisible income tax. It similarly raised railway fares instead of imposing a railway tax that would be shared with States. These arrangements must be reconsidered, and I expect cooperation from members of every party.

There is no party politics in this. It will not prevent you from returning to government; indeed, you may return to a financially stronger State. You boast that you created revenue before handing office to us. Should I not have the honour of adding what we can and handing it to you if you return? Cooperate now; decide later who should govern.

Personally, I am not greatly attached to office. Celebrate a liberation day or deliverance day when I leave if you wish. I regard 6 March last year as the day my freedom disappeared, not the day I acquired new status. Use your skill as you please and build strength to return; I will not grieve.

It was said that a procession led by Mr Ponnappa Nadar smashed pots. No one should think breaking a pot can break this Government; only the pot breaks. He may not enjoy doing it, but if he refused, someone might say a more prolific pot-breaker should lead the procession. Mothers teach children not to break pots, overturn vessels, or spill milk because these are sinful acts, but political fashion may compel him.

I told my party friends not to be upset. If Congress holds a pot-breaking procession one day, they may hold one another day and break pots while demanding that the Delhi Government, which would not provide the ten crore rupees needed for subsidised rice, be condemned. Even if no other industry grows, alternating pot-breaking processions will at least develop that village industry! (Laughter.) Such actions neither anger nor distress me; I only consider them unnecessary.

After the Leader of the Opposition has wished us another four years, why break pots? If others do so despite his blessing, perhaps they lack faith in their leader. (Laughter.)

Mr Poovaragan said we failed to provide subsidised rice everywhere. I openly admit my regret; I do not hide it or ask, “We could not do it, so what?” I want to expand it as conditions improve. My only sorrow is that when I consider stepping aside for virtuous people who can do it, those virtuous people say they cannot either.

**P. G. Karuthiruman:** We did not say that.

**Annadurai:** They say they never said it. When we fulfil even a quarter or half-quarter of what we promised, dismissing it as nothing recalls the mother-in-law and daughter-in-law story. The daughter-in-law told a beggar, “There is no alms; go.” The mother-in-law called him back only to say, “She has no authority to refuse you. I say there is nothing; now go.” What benefit does the country gain if you return merely to say, “There is no subsidised rice; go”?

Cooperate more with procurement and increased production. Offer constructive ideas. If we procure adequately and you join us in seeking some financial assistance from the Union Government, I can try to extend the subsidised-rice scheme to more places.

Mr Poovaragan said the people were disappointed. I openly agree, and I myself am disappointed. I had expected to extend the scheme somewhat this year. Doubts have arisen over whether food production will reach the expected level. This year we must concentrate particularly on both production and availability, ensuring sufficient output and uninterrupted access for all. The water level at Mettur has fallen; I ask Congress members to reread that passage in the statement.

Mr Karuthiruman asks what we said when the monsoon was lower in 1965–66. Suppose we spoke unwisely through lack of experience. Must their twenty years of experience be used to repeat the same mistake? (Laughter.) If we erred, use your experience to point it out. Tell us constructively how to prevent agricultural decline during a poor monsoon. With cooperation, higher production, successful procurement and some Union aid, I will not hesitate to extend the rice scheme.

[Image 19 is almost wholly illegible in the OCR. It appears between the appeal for cooperation on rice and the renewed discussion of whether the scheme should continue. No continuous sentence is recoverable, so no text is invented.]

In speeches you say that if subsidised rice cannot be provided everywhere, we should abandon it; yet in some places you yourselves demand it. If you believe the scheme is wrong, say so boldly. *The Hindu* openly writes that it should not exist, and I welcome that candour. Will Congress members say the same? No stable answer comes. You waver because the public may ask why you oppose something others are at least providing.

Whether or not subsidised rice is supplied, we must reason in terms of economic justice. You ask for prices to fall. Have they not fallen? Six months ago you asked about the price of pulses and tamarind; now those questions have disappeared.

**K. Vinayagam:** Through whose efforts did the price of tamarind fall? Was that too by this Government's grace?

**Annadurai:** Through the efforts of the tamarind tree! (Laughter.) Not by this Government's grace, but the tamarind tree's. When prices rise gradually someone must accept responsibility, and the Union Government must accept the role of “major partner.” When I mocked rising prices in the past, some of those now opposite joined me in the mockery. I remember it well.

Price restraint can be attempted only in relation to the Union Government's inflationary policy. The Union Budget now has a deficit exceeding Rs 290 crore. Mr Karuthiruman questions why one State item has fallen from a cited amount, but what does he say of this deficit? The Indian Finance Minister's English assurances from the previous month are destroyed in OCR; the surrounding question is whether he fulfilled them. We do not demand why every prediction failed, but his own account should be remembered.

Mr Karuthiruman asked what pride there was in calling this a tax-free budget. Not one of its fifty-eight pages claims that. If ruling-party members take satisfaction in calling it tax-free, what loss does that cause you? Can you not tolerate even that?

He cited earlier taxes on jaggery and other items, totalling about Rs 1 crore, and an increase in agricultural income tax. That increase followed agreement in the Select Committee and was not expected to yield much additional revenue. The Delhi Budget imposes Rs 65 crore in taxes, of which our State may receive only about Rs 1 crore. Had railway tax been raised, we might have received more, but other taxes were chosen instead.

When I say “we” and “our State,” please forget political parties at least on financial questions. I ask you to unite with us in arguing with the Union Government; I do not even say fighting it. My ministerial colleagues answered detailed points while I was absent. I thank the House for allowing me to join the general debate and offer these explanations, and ask members to support the Budget.

**Honourable Speaker:** An announcement. Printed notes for the debate on demands for grants, incorporating the correction schedule, have been distributed to members. Debate will proceed from tomorrow morning through the twenty-sixth of this month. Today's business is concluded. The House will meet tomorrow at about 9 a.m.

The House was then adjourned.

---NOTES---
The scan identifies this as Speech 35, delivered on 6 March 1968 during the general debate on the 1968–69 Budget Statement. The stray OCR phrase “அதிஷ்ட உ ய!” is not present as a title in the scan and has not been used as one.

The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the final bilingual document. Image 19 is almost entirely unreadable, while several English quotations in Images 6, 10, 11, 12 and 21 were processed with a Tamil-only OCR model and are destroyed. Their speakers and recoverable surrounding arguments are translated, but the quotations are not reconstructed.

All Third Plan targets, achievements, tax amounts and deficit figures are historical source claims. They should be verified against the scans and official economic records before statistical citation.

“படி அரிசி” refers to the DMK's subsidised measure-based rice promise and is translated as the subsidised-rice scheme rather than literally as “one measure of rice.”

The gunny-cloth pregnancy, orphan, mother-in-law, sword-grip, pot-breaking and tamarind-tree passages are rhetorical stories or jokes. Their political functions and humour are retained without treating them as factual incidents.
